{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-110367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{ }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}+2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,2)=-1\\cdot 2=-2≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$f$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$h$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x)}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x-y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(0,1)=0-1=-1≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ only"] ["text" "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$"] ["text" "$g$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$h$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{xy}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nh(0,2)=\\frac{0-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$f$ only"] ["text" "$g$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f$ and $g$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/268e469b0306d22e6b6dc05905a9d56a1cabd4b0e405f741ff458ca241440d4a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]_{y=e}^{y={e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/01f3a8d99dd38ce2a728fe2cb4e4ab8de933ccd9ba27c87174779c428c303f48.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/a49e679b3919594edc7fe963190a754324b9b5ea980d1936121c040d0ee52284.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nW\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c(1-xy),\\, & {y}^{2}\\le x\\le y, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/1ef0c7b289e24a98366c64b9a213c4f67d2fc63157b3319f0118eb16c514097e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx-\\frac{cy{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & =c{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}-\\frac{c}{8}-\\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{c}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}cx{e}^{y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/8a94024cbce0389b1d9c60bf64a38cb7c8e8e52836b2b39e9e638f856a1c5269.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{e}^{y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{3}-\\frac{4c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{3}-\\frac{c}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-110987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-110987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y>1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X+Y>1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/58c1beb1b13680e330260d93fdf8e60460e2bfeae4c1cbbc2fce8b261afe7ea1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-111167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.3"] ["math" "0.5"] ["math" "0.6"] ["math" "0.75"] ["math" "0.8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<Y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A={X<Y}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/9213a4b132f625677fcc5719e71900d037ac08088dc477f8d6a93f9ca67bc3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{xy}{4}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{xy}{4}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{8}]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-111150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{32}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<1).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ),$ and the event $A={X+Y<1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/df0bf32b4785a9f43946bd3b08f1b255549485f2275c74607fb5285e2b1baa3c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{e}^{-x-y}]_{x=0}^{x=1-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{-y}-{e}^{-1})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}-{e}^{-1}y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{e} \\\\  & ≈0.264.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-111153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.132"] ["math" "0.264"] ["math" "0.375"] ["math" "0.421"] ["math" "0.528"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.264"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & 0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D={0\\le y\\le x},$ and the event $A={X+Y<2}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/f7a0f0fb9d20a4b88d0aa4c395e2adb1d62195601ce756f57a48becc6ab38681.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y<2) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{2}{(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(1+2y)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{9})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{1+2y}-\\frac{2y}{9}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-111163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{27}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X>1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/26cbd44827c3a5a3fde5d61b631ce4f214bcf9a36ce6bf76129b1fe9f9478eff.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3aba8af1aed06b91aafd7083c4793255ecb93669459e4a5eaa5cad9c686d01a6"} {"question" "q-111168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "q-111168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7def2264a6fcda72c30a7435411e598bd64199b6a7bf41c2754e2ec6e630676"} {"question" "e-9207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/a1b3940558e5a6d90d2911c18571eb91d63a4baa63bbe467fb8359c6fa5f44ea.png)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/example-9207.json" "file_sha256" "5e6f0ab33b7ac5902bfc9cbc92096a4c3d886237326adbff856d215d6b908e47"} {"question" "e-9207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\:  0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/ccb7cc52f8404dcca5d555d4fd81e6ae03e9689ccdd7c462fccbc2dc611db235.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =c{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =c[(1-0)-(0-1)] \\\\  & =c[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/example-9207.json" "file_sha256" "5e6f0ab33b7ac5902bfc9cbc92096a4c3d886237326adbff856d215d6b908e47"} {"question" "e-9183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/bba3d767c1001256f5bd4bd9bbf38059054dee21ff51714f1d3ed463f431a6c3.png)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/example-9183.json" "file_sha256" "e3cb8c170dc6fd260ff53befcf1c50cca7e82c862f6740e4ffb98550ce2c16d9"} {"question" "e-9183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/9c823d67872095514c6392038ff43497512de656e28b26160465760df0faa4ea.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }(1+x+y)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }0-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{c}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+2x)^{-1}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}{[\\frac{1}{1+2x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/example-9183.json" "file_sha256" "e3cb8c170dc6fd260ff53befcf1c50cca7e82c862f6740e4ffb98550ce2c16d9"} {"question" "e-9184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/f7aed2445b7b518afecd13a5979a873b8bfc17e8eb1ff8d41f4a0d9c912deaf4.png)\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/example-9184.json" "file_sha256" "3dd9847dfd8cc9ede1b91cfec21d7d4c592fb4f6caf7b39f67cb95de8d8aff09"} {"question" "e-9184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D = [0,3]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A = \\left\\{ X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/b1fc6548c909b9887c708c4c5dcce5b715d112defc7ce1fc639784bbaf55e111.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1,Y<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/example-9184.json" "file_sha256" "3dd9847dfd8cc9ede1b91cfec21d7d4c592fb4f6caf7b39f67cb95de8d8aff09"}] "usage" {"e-9207" [] "e-9184" [] "q-110366" [] "e-9183" [] "q-110367" []} "basis" 2057 "retractions" [[17592186162231 :question/problem "Let $D = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$. Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n1. $f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n2. $g(x, y) = {-xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n3. $h(x, y) = {\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$"] [17592186162230 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = {cxy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le y < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$."] [17592186161810 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${c\\sin (x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < \\frac{π}{2}, 0 < y < \\frac{π}{2} \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$."] [17592186161810 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/f9bda8492314ef0374cbb01c5b669ef2.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n$∫_{0}^{π/2}[∫_{0}^{π/2}c\\sin (x + y)dy]dx|= c∫_{0}^{π/2}[∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (x + y)dy]dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}[- \\cos (x + y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/2}dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}(-\\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2}) + \\cos x)dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx$\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n$c∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx|= c[\\sin x - \\sin (x + \\frac{π}{2})]_{0}^{π/2}; = c[(\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\sin π) - (\\sin (0) - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}))]; = c[(1 - 0) - (0 - 1)]; = c[1 - (-1)]; = 2c$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186161804 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x < y \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$."] [17592186161804 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/f7c3d4d867e4cd8c83125f9b879c525e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}[∫_{x}^{∞}(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3})dy]dx &= c∫_{0}^{∞}[∫_{x}^{∞}(1 + x + y)^{-3}dy]dx \\\\\n&= c∫_{0}^{∞}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}[(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}0 - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞} - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n> Then, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n> $\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx|= \\frac{c}{2}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 2x)^{-1}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[\\frac{1}{1 + 2x}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[0 - 1]; = \\frac{c}{4}$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4\n$$"] [17592186161805 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{6},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1)$."] [17592186161805 :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2$, then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region\n\n$$\nD = [0,3]\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\nand the event\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to a subset of $D$, shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/a5d3eeb749188c014bfe84991e95ddc8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 1, Y < 1) &= ∬_{A}f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∬_{A}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}Area(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (3 - 1) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186158923 17592186158924 17592186158925 17592186158926 17592186158927 17592186158929 17592186158930 17592186158931 17592186158932 17592186158933] "immutable_fields" [[17592186158922 :answer-field/correct] [17592186158922 :answer-field/id] [17592186158922 :answer-field/key] [17592186158922 :answer-field/type] [17592186158928 :answer-field/correct] [17592186158928 :answer-field/id] [17592186158928 :answer-field/key] [17592186158928 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
